Day 15 — Nmap Scanning Techniques 

Today I continued my hands-on learning with Nmap, focusing on different scanning techniques, firewall-aware scanning, host discovery, service detection, and scan analysis.

  Topics Covered
SYN, ACK, UDP & ARP Scans
  Understanding different Nmap scan methods
  How scans behave with firewalls and filtered ports
  ARP-based host discovery on local networks
ICMP Timestamp, Traceroute & DNS Resolution
  Understanding ICMP-based discovery
  Using traceroute to analyze network paths
  DNS resolution during Nmap scans
Scanning Linux-Based Machines
  Performing host and port discovery
  Identifying open, closed, and filtered ports
Port Specification & Scan Order
  Selecting specific ports and port ranges
  Understanding how Nmap determines scan order
Nmap Scan Techniques
  -sS — SYN Scan
  -sT — TCP Connect Scan
  -sA — ACK Scan
  -sW — Window Scan
  -sM — Maimon Scan
OS & Service Detection
  -O — OS detection
  -sV — Service/version detection
  -A — Aggressive scan
  UDP range scanning
  Diagnosing and interpreting scan results
Nmap Output & Verbosity
  Understanding different output formats
  Using verbosity to obtain more detailed scan information
    
    
    Hands-On Practice

I practiced these techniques in my authorized lab environment to understand how Nmap identifies hosts, ports, services, operating systems, and network behavior.

  Key Takeaway

Nmap is more than just a port scanner. Understanding scan techniques, firewall behavior, service detection, OS fingerprinting, and result diagnosis is essential for effective network reconnaissance and cybersecurity.

